# Mutual TLS Example

By default, tunnel connections are encrypted and authenticated using ephemeral self-signed certificates generated at startup. No configuration is required.

For persistent deployments — or where you need to control the root of trust and rotate certificates independently — you can supply your own certificates. This example walks through generating a CA, issuing node certificates, and passing them to entry and exit nodes.

## Generating Certificates

Generate a self-signed CA to act as the root of trust:

```bash
openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:4096 -days 365 -nodes \
  -keyout ca-key.pem -out ca-cert.pem \
  -subj "/CN=wallhack-ca"
```

Generate a node certificate signed by that CA:

```bash
openssl req -newkey rsa:4096 -nodes \
  -keyout node-key.pem -out node-req.pem \
  -subj "/CN=wallhack-node"

openssl x509 -req -in node-req.pem -days 365 \
  -CA ca-cert.pem -CAkey ca-key.pem -CAcreateserial \
  -out node-cert.pem
```

## Starting the Tunnel

Pass the certificate, key, and CA to both nodes using `--cert`, `--key`, and `--ca`. Each node presents its certificate and verifies the other against the shared CA.

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443 --cert node-cert.pem --key node-key.pem --ca ca-cert.pem" title="Entry node" /%}

{% codeblock code="wallhack exit --connect ENTRY:443 --cert node-cert.pem --key node-key.pem --ca ca-cert.pem" title="Exit node" /%}

{% callout type="tip" %}
Issue unique certificates per node rather than sharing one. This lets you revoke a single node without regenerating the entire chain.
{% /callout %}
